# Git-level line-ending normalization (last-resort CRLF defence; the IDE .editorconfig
# is the first layer). Ensures Java and other text sources are stored and checked out
# with LF regardless of the developer's OS or core.autocrlf setting.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.java   text eol=lf
*.xml    text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF.
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.bat    text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never normalize.
*.jar    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.gif    binary
*.p12    binary
*.jks    binary

# Flyway migrations are checksummed artifacts, not ordinary source: Core reports a Java
# migration's checksum from DatabaseMigration.JavaMigrationChecksums and Flyway derives a SQL
# migration's from the file itself, both validated against the value stored when the migration
# ran. -text keeps git from rewriting a single byte of them, so no checkout, renormalisation or
# core.autocrlf setting can ever disturb a shipped migration. Spotless and Checkstyle skip the
# same paths - see the plugin excludes in pom.xml.
src/main/java/db/migration/**       -text
src/main/resources/db/migration/**  -text
